One, two! One, two! The vorpal blade went snicker-snack!
He left it dead and with its head he went galumphing back.

But I think Snickersnee comes from The Mikado (hence the Japanese 
connection) by Gilbert and Sullivan.  It was the name of a sword.

As The Mikado followed Through the Looking Glass by 13 years (1872 & 1885),
it is possible that Jabberwocky was an influence, but I doubt it.
Snickersnee and Snicker-snack are fairly obvious onomatopoetic words
for the sibilant swishing sound of a sword (read this last phrase aloud).
